7] [17592186494995 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494995 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494995 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a47a029c-812d-5ac8-b753-06da557530dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495050 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02602311-35ef-53b7-9d8d-d64db15f3075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495050 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}))+(x)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}))}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})+x\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{(2x)^{2}})) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x})-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{(\\frac{1}{2x})}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1) \\\\  & =-{(2x)}^{-x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495099 #edb/ref 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "743cb560-d24b-5701-9107-2d76831f26b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495099 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $1+t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt-\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495110 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495110 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495110 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495110 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b4/b420afa755a7cd99e427fc7c1aa06ea745f0a177fa83c60dff5a1bcd354c7b79.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495110 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bd4a768-e4d0-520a-9786-1699a6b9a16d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495110 #edb/ref 1074 "The solid is a **rectangular pyramid** since it has a rectangular base and the other $4$ triangular faces meet at a single point.   \n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a cube since not all of its faces are squares.\n\n- The solid is not a triangular prism because it does not have two triangular bases connected by parallel line segments.\n\n- The solid is not a triangular pyramid since its base is not triangular.\n\n- The solid is not a heptagonal pyramid since its base is not a heptagon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495152 #edb/ref 1071 "An eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =3k-2$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbb0dde9-c40d-5e25-859f-e2de0b0b8937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495152 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{aligned}] & =(3k-2)[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -30+35k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6(3k-2) \\\\ (5k)(3k-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -30+35k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-18k+12 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-10k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=-18k+12 \\\\ -30+35k=15{k}^{2}-10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-6=-18k+12\\,⟹\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30+35k & =15{k}^{2}-10k \\\\ -30+35\\cdot 1 & =15\\cdot {1}^{2}-10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 5 & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495153 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd432b0a-1c26-5d37-8911-e5b811490135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495153 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{1}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{9}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+5}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{9},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y+5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=-5:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1+9t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y+5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=-5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{9},\\,y=-5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495176 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495176 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "028bab25-3fed-5252-aa96-72f7de3058fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495176 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186323750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495177 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495177 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0324c12e-b94a-5be1-b1ff-869be8d13ba6" #edb/ref